-- Objects, missiles and turrets, which scripts hold by handles.

declare class Object
    -- The slot it fills in the mission, from 0.
    slot: number
    -- Its type, such as `predator`.
    type: ShipType
    -- Its class, such as `fighter`; nil for an object without stats, such as a nav point.
    class: ShipClass?
    -- The pilot flying it, whose record sets how it flies and fights: a pilot of the game's by its number, one a mod adds by its qualified name, or `none`.
    pilot: PilotNumber
    -- The side it's on. Setting it changes its side, as a mission's SetHostile does.
    side: Side
    -- Where it is. Setting it moves it there at once, as a mission's SnapToPoint does.
    position: vector
    -- Where its axes point: to its right, down and forward, out of its nose (`openreliant.util`). Setting it turns it at once, its axes made unit length and at right angles first, the forward one keeping its direction.
    orientation: Orientation
    -- How far it moves in a simulation step, of which there are 25 a second. Setting it pushes it, and its engines carry on from there.
    velocity: vector
    -- How fast it moves: the length of its velocity.
    speed: number
    -- How far its model reaches from its middle.
    radius: number
    -- Whether it's the player's ship.
    is_player: boolean
    -- The order it's following: one of the game's (`Order`), or a mod's by its qualified name; nil for none.
    order: (string | number)?
    -- What the order it's following is aimed at; nil while it follows none.
    target: Target?
    -- The object that last hit it; nil for none, or once that one has left the mission.
    last_attacker: Object?
    -- Its throttle: 1 is full, 2 the afterburner's and -1 reverse thrust's. Its order or its pilot usually sets it each frame.
    throttle: number
    -- How hard it rolls, from -1 to 1. Its order or its pilot usually sets it each frame.
    roll_input: number
    -- How hard it pitches, from -1 to 1. Its order or its pilot usually sets it each frame.
    pitch_input: number
    -- How hard it yaws, from -1 to 1. Its order or its pilot usually sets it each frame.
    yaw_input: number
    -- Whether its afterburner burns.
    afterburning: boolean
    -- The afterburner's fuel left, in seconds of burning. Setting it fills or drains the tank, from 0 up.
    afterburner_fuel: number
    -- How many countermeasures it has left.
    countermeasures: number
    -- Its shields in each quadrant. Each can be set from 0 to what a whole ship of its type has; an object without stats has no shields to set.
    shields: Quadrants
    -- Its armour in each quadrant. Each can be set from 0 to what a whole ship of its type has, and its guns, speed and shields' recharge follow, as damage wears them; an object without stats has no armour to set.
    armor: Quadrants
    -- The share of its armour it has left, from about 1 as it's made down to 0: its weakest quadrant against a quadrant's full armour. Nil for an object without stats.
    hull: number?
    -- What can harm it: anything for `none`, nothing for `full`, only a player's ship for `player_can_hit`, and anything for `eject_before_exploding`, though its pilot ejects first. Setting it is what a mission's SetInvulnerability does.
    invulnerable: Invulnerability
    -- Whether it has started to explode. It takes no more orders.
    exploding: boolean
    -- Whether its pilot has ejected. It takes no more orders.
    ejected: boolean
    -- Whether it's cloaked. Setting it cloaks or uncloaks it, as a mission's Cloak does, where its model can.
    cloaked: boolean
    -- Whether ships can target it. Setting it is what a mission's SetTargetable does: a type that can't be targeted stays so.
    targetable: boolean
    -- Whether its lights are on. Setting it is what a mission's DisableLights does.
    lights: boolean
    -- Whether it's left out of the mission's work, as a mission's DisableObject leaves it.
    disabled: boolean
    -- Whether its guns don't fire and its turrets rest, as a mission's DisableGuns sets.
    guns_disabled: boolean
    -- Whether it can't launch missiles, as a mission's DisableMissiles sets.
    missiles_disabled: boolean
    -- Whether its engines are off: its throttle held at 0, and no afterburner or reverse thrust, as a mission's DisableEngines sets.
    engines_disabled: boolean
    -- Whether the player can't eject from it, as a mission's DisableEject sets.
    eject_disabled: boolean
    -- Whether it keeps to its orders: it doesn't retaliate, come to another's help, rise to a taunt or take the wingmen's commands, as a mission's DoNotDisturb sets.
    do_not_disturb: boolean
    -- Whether it no longer keeps clear of other ships, as a mission's SetShipAvoidance sets.
    avoidance_disabled: boolean
    -- Whether the object is still in the mission. A handle stops being valid once its object is removed or its mission ends.
    function is_valid(self): boolean
    -- Gives it `order`, aimed at `target` or at nothing, as a mission's SetAI does: the order goes on top of its orders if the one it follows gives way. `component` aims it at one part of `target` instead of the whole ship, as a mission's orders can: for Launch, the carrier's launch gate, counting from 0; for Dock, the port. Returns whether it took. Global scripts can give any object orders, and an object's scripts their own object.
    function give_order(self, order: string | number, target: Object?, component: number?): boolean
    -- Starts its Launch, as a mission's StartLaunch does: the first Launch among its orders goes after the short random wait the game gives each ship. Returns whether it had a Launch to start. Global scripts can start any object's launch, and an object's scripts their own.
    function start_launch(self): boolean
    -- Sends the event `name` to the object's scripts, with `data`, which must be plain data. It arrives at the next update.
    function send_event(self, name: string, data: any): ()
    -- Starts the script `name` of the calling mod on the object, as an object script, and passes `data` to its `on_init`. Returns whether it started. Only global scripts can add scripts.
    function add_script(self, name: string, data: any?): boolean
    -- Stops the script `name` of the calling mod on the object. Returns whether it ran there. Only global scripts can remove scripts.
    function remove_script(self, name: string): boolean
    -- `hooks.add`, for the calls that concern this object only: a handler for the hook `name`, with an optional `filter`. Returns the handler's handle. Global scripts can hook any object, and an object's scripts their own.
    function hook(self, name: string, handler: (e: any) -> boolean?, filter: (Filter | (e: any) -> boolean)?): HookHandle
    -- Draws the object with the surface function `name`, the calling mod's by its own name or any mod's by the qualified one, reading `parameters`; nil draws it with its textures' functions again. Returns false if no function of that name is registered. Only player scripts can set it.
    function set_surface(self, name: string?, parameters: { number }?): boolean
    -- Its turrets: its guns that turn to aim, spin their barrels or launch missiles, destroyed ones included, in the order of its guns.
    function turrets(self): { Turret }
end
declare class Missile
    -- Its type, such as `raptor`, or the qualified name of one a mod adds.
    type: MissileType
    -- The object that launched it, or let it fall; nil once that one has left the mission.
    launcher: Object?
    -- What it flies at. Its guidance turns to a new target from the next frame.
    target: Target
    -- The side it's on: its launcher's, as it was launched.
    side: Side
    -- Where it is. Setting it moves it there at once.
    position: vector
    -- Where its axes point: to its right, down and forward, out of its nose (`openreliant.util`). Setting it turns it at once, its axes made unit length and at right angles first, and its guidance turns on from there.
    orientation: Orientation
    -- How far it moves in a simulation step, of which there are 25 a second. Setting it pushes it, and its motor carries on from there.
    velocity: vector
    -- How fast it moves: the length of its velocity.
    speed: number
    -- Whether the missile is still in flight. A handle stops being valid once its flight ends or its mission ends.
    function is_valid(self): boolean
    -- Ends its flight now, as it ends when its time runs out: it blows up where it is, with a shockwave for a Havoc or an Imp. Global scripts can set off any missile, and a missile's own scripts their missile.
    function detonate(self): ()
end
declare class Turret
    -- The object it's on.
    object: Object
    -- What it is: `aimed`, which turns to aim at a target of its own; `spinning`, a gun whose barrels spin up while its object's trigger is held; or `launcher`, which launches missiles. Nil once it's destroyed.
    kind: TurretKind?
    -- Whether it was destroyed with its base. It turns and fires no more.
    destroyed: boolean
    -- The type of the shots it fires; nil for a missile turret, or once it's destroyed.
    gun_type: GunType?
    -- Where its base is; nil once it's destroyed.
    position: vector?
    -- What it aims at: an aimed turret's and a missile turret's own target; nil for a spinning gun, which fires where its object points, or once it's destroyed. Setting it aims the turret, which turns to it, and nil leaves it to look for one.
    target: Target?
    -- Whether its object is still in the mission. A handle stops being valid once its object is removed or its mission ends.
    function is_valid(self): boolean
end

-- The records (`openreliant.records`).

declare class Ship
    name: string
    max_speed: number
    inertia: number
    yaw_rate: number
    yaw_inertia: number
    pitch_rate: number
    pitch_inertia: number
    roll_rate: number
    roll_inertia: number
    shield_power: number
    armor_class: number
    afterburner_fuel: number
    shield_recharge: number
    gun_energy: number
    gun_recharge: number
    rounds: number
end
declare class ShipTypeRecord
    targetable: boolean
    name: number
    class: ShipClass
    side: Side
    display: TargetForm
end
declare class Gun
    name: string
    range: number
    speed: number
    damage: Damage
    fire_rate: number
    shot_energy: number
end
declare class Damage
    shield: number
    hull: number
end
declare class Missile
    name: string
    speed: number
    turn_rate: number
    flight_time: number
    damage: Damage
    lock_time: number
    decoy_chance: number
    lock_range: number
    component_damage: number
end
declare class Pilot
    name: string
    tier_a: PilotTier
    tier_b: PilotTier
    tier_c: PilotTier
    skill: PilotSkill
end
declare class Face
    name: number
    talking: string
    laughing: string
    squadron: string
    dying: string
end
declare class CampaignMission
    hologram: string?
    speech: string?
    last_word: string?
    carrier: Carrier
    objectives: { string }
    date: string?
end
type Records = {
    ships: { [number | string]: Ship },
    ship_types: { [number | string]: ShipTypeRecord },
    guns: { [number | string]: Gun },
    missiles: { [number | string]: Missile },
    pilots: { [number | string]: Pilot },
    faces: { [number | string]: Face },
    text: { [number | string]: string },
    itac_text: { [number | string]: string },
    -- The campaign's missions, by their numbers, in the order it flies them. Load scripts change it by assigning a new list.
    campaign: { number },
    -- What the campaign makes of each of its missions, by its number. Load scripts change their fields.
    missions: { [number]: CampaignMission },
}

-- The packages made from declarations. `openreliant.self` is the script's own Object.

-- OpenReliant's version, events for the global scripts, and game modes.
type Core = {
    -- The version of OpenReliant, such as `0.7.0`.
    version: string,
    -- The qualified name of the game mode that runs, such as `arena:arena`; nil in the game's campaign, INSTANT ACTION and anywhere else.
    game_mode: string?,
    -- The mission the game mode that runs is at: the one flown, or between missions the one flown next. nil where no game mode runs.
    game_mode_mission: GameModeMission?,
    -- Registers a game mode, which the main menu's GAME MODES lists. The mod's name qualifies its `name`, and the screen shows its `label` and `description`. `missions` lists the missions it flies in turn: each is the number of a standard `.DTE` file of the game's or a mod's, or a table that also gives the number the mission flies as, the names of its objectives, and its `hologram` and `last_word` in the briefing room. `ship` is the ship the player flies them in; without it, each mission gives the ship. With `loop`, the mode starts again after its last mission. A `campaign` shows the restart screen after a mission is lost or left, and carries on from the mission the player reached. `briefing` names the mod's registered screen that the front end shows before each mission. `briefing_room`, `reliant` or `yamato`, then briefs each mission in that game's briefing room, with the loadout. `loadout_ships` lists the ships that loadout offers, in order, starting on the first. `wing_pilots` lists the pilots of the player's wingmen, Alpha 2 to 6, in place of the campaign's wing, which the missions flown as the campaign's numbers give the wingmen: each a pilot of the game's by its number, or one a mod adds by its qualified name, and `none` keeps a place's pilot. With `debriefing`, the ITAC debriefs each mission that goes on to the next. `ending` names the mod's registered screen that the front end shows after the last mission. `records` names the mod's script that changes the records for the mode's missions alone: it runs as a load script before each of them, and its changes go back as the mission ends. Only load and menu scripts can use it, as OpenReliant starts. Returns the mode's qualified name.
    register_game_mode: (definition: GameMode) -> string,
    -- Sends the event `name` to the global and mission scripts, with `data`, which must be plain data. It arrives at the next update.
    send_global_event: (name: string, data: any) -> (),
}

-- The mission's objects, the player's ship and the mission itself.
type World = {
    -- The player's ship, while a mission runs; nil between missions.
    player: Object?,
    -- The mission that runs, with its `number` and its `file`'s name; nil between missions.
    mission: Mission?,
    -- Every object in the mission, in the order of their slots.
    objects: () -> { Object },
    -- Every missile in flight, newest first.
    missiles: () -> { Missile },
}

-- The objects around the script's own object or missile.
type Nearby = {
    -- The objects within `radius` of the script's object or missile, or of the player's ship for a player script, nearest first, without it.
    objects: (radius: number) -> { Object },
}

-- What the order table says of each order, the orders each object has, and ending them. An object's give_order gives orders.
type Orders = {
    -- Registers an order, which `name` qualified with the mod's name names. `update` runs each frame on each ship that follows it, and returns false to end the order; `init` runs as it starts, and `exit` as it ends. They can't give or end orders. Returns the qualified name, which `give_order` and `orders.info` take. The order goes away when the scripts that registered it stop.
    register: (name: string, definition: {priority: number?, flags: OrderFlags?, init: ((ship: Object, target: Object?, seconds: number) -> ())?, update: (ship: Object, target: Object?, seconds: number) -> boolean?, exit: ((ship: Object, target: Object?, seconds: number) -> ())?}) -> string,
    -- The name, priority and flags of `order`: one of the game's, the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one. Nil for an order that doesn't exist, or a mod's that failed.
    info: (order: string | number) -> OrderInfo?,
    -- The orders `object` has, the one it follows first, each with what it's aimed at. The ones below carry on as each ends.
    stack: (object: Object) -> { OrderEntry },
    -- Ends the order `object` follows, as an order ends itself: its exit runs, and the order below it carries on. Returns whether it had one. Global scripts can end any object's orders, and an object's scripts their own object's.
    cancel: (object: Object) -> boolean,
    -- Drops all of `object`'s orders, as a mission's ClearAI does, where the one it follows gives way. Returns whether they were dropped. Global scripts can drop any object's orders, and an object's scripts their own object's.
    clear: (object: Object) -> boolean,
}

-- Lines said on the radio, as a mission's comms commands say them.
type Radio = {
    -- The ship `ship` says the speech file `speech` on the radio, a file of the game's or a mod's such as `ms_dice22_001.ut`, its pilot's face showing, as a mission's CommsFromShip does: at once, ending the line playing, unless `line` says otherwise. The line goes through the `radio_say` hook. A ship being destroyed, or a stand-in, says nothing. Returns whether the mission's radio took the line: false between missions.
    say: (ship: Object, speech: string, line: RadioLine?) -> boolean,
    -- Pilot `pilot` says the speech file `speech` on the radio, with its face, as a mission's CommsFromPilot does: at once, ending the line playing, unless `line` says otherwise. The pilot is one of the game's by its name or number, or one a mod adds by its qualified name. The line goes through the `radio_say` hook. Returns whether the mission's radio took the line: false between missions.
    say_pilot: (pilot: PilotNumber, speech: string, line: RadioLine?) -> boolean,
}

-- Drawing over the flight display, while it's shown: text, lines and rectangles, in the window's pixels.
type Hud = {
    -- The game's instruments the mods' displays stand in for this frame, which aren't drawn (`register_display`).
    replaced: { HudInstrument },
    -- Whether the game's instruments show this frame: during a mission, in the view ahead from the cockpit.
    instruments_shown: boolean,
    -- The player's guns as the gunnery window and the targeting cluster show them; nil outside a mission.
    guns: HudGuns?,
    -- The player's missiles as the missile window shows them; nil outside a mission.
    missiles: HudMissiles?,
    -- The target the display shows, with its subtarget as `component`; nil for none, or outside a mission.
    target: Target?,
    -- What the target display shows of its target, in either form, whether or not its window is open; nil without a target or while the display hides it, and outside a mission.
    target_display: HudTargetDisplay?,
    -- The radar: its range, and the contacts it shows; nil outside a mission.
    radar: HudRadar?,
    -- The kills the skull readout shows; nil outside a mission.
    kills: number?,
    -- The seconds of afterburner fuel the fuel readout shows; nil outside a mission.
    fuel: number?,
    -- The countermeasures the coil readout shows; nil outside a mission.
    countermeasures: number?,
    -- Whether the countermeasures readout was lit as the display last drew it: always in the view ahead from the cockpit, except while it's dark in a flash that `ShowHudIcon` sets; false outside the view ahead, and outside a mission.
    countermeasures_lit: boolean,
    -- The targeting cluster about the middle of the screen, as it shows the player's speed, throttle and guns; nil outside a mission.
    gauges: HudGauges?,
    -- The ship status indicator, as it shows the player's shields and armour; nil for a ship without them, or outside a mission.
    ship_status: HudShipStatus?,
    -- The status lights that show, steady or flashing, in the order the display packs them; none outside a mission.
    lights: { HudLight },
    -- The status lights lit as the display last drew them, in the order it packs them: those of `lights`, but a flashing one only while it's lit. A warning that flashes keeps its place in the grid while it's dark; a light that `ShowHudIcon` flashes gives its place up. None outside the view ahead from the cockpit, where the display draws no lights, and outside a mission.
    lights_lit: { HudLight },
    -- The charges of the player's ECM, cloak and spectral shields, which their lights show as bars under them, each from 0 to 1, or nil where the ship doesn't carry the device; nil outside a mission.
    charges: HudCharges?,
    -- The mission's clock as the display shows it: the countdown where the mission counts down, and the time played otherwise; nil outside a mission.
    clock: HudClock?,
    -- The view's name the display writes at the top of the screen, in the views it names; nil in the others, the view ahead from the cockpit among them, and outside a mission.
    view_name: string?,
    -- The date the launch types out at the foot of the screen, as far as it has typed it; nil while it isn't shown, and outside a mission.
    caption: string?,
    -- The damage window, as it shows how well the player's weapons, engines and shields still work as the armour wears, each from 0 to 1, whether or not the window is open; nil outside a mission.
    damage: HudDamage?,
    -- The power window, as it shows the shields', guns' and engines' shares of the player's power, as the whole percentages it writes, whether or not the window is open; nil outside a mission.
    power: HudPower?,
    -- The ships of the player's wing the wing status window shows, the player's first, whether or not the window is open; none outside a mission.
    wingmen: { HudWingman },
    -- The objectives window: the mission's objectives it can show, and the one it shows, whether or not the window is open; nil outside a mission.
    objectives: HudObjectives?,
    -- The items of the radio's menu the comms window lists, in order, as the number keys pick them, whether or not the window is open; none outside a mission.
    comms: { string },
    -- The message lines the display shows, oldest first; none outside a mission.
    messages: { string },
    -- The line `DisplaySubTitle` shows near the foot of the screen in the director's view, whichever view it's in; nil for none, and outside a mission.
    subtitle: string?,
    -- The action whose key `WaitForKey`'s prompt asks the player to press; nil while nothing waits, and outside a mission.
    key_prompt: Action?,
    -- The prompt that flashes in the view ahead for what the mission has ready: the warp's or the jump's; nil for none, and outside a mission.
    jump_prompt: HudJumpPrompt?,
    -- The game's windows that are open, opening or closing (`window_state`); none outside a mission.
    open_windows: { HudInstrument },
    -- Whether it's shown this frame, which is when what's drawn on it shows, and its other fields can be read.
    shown: boolean,
    -- The window's width, in pixels.
    width: number,
    -- The window's height, in pixels.
    height: number,
    -- Registers a display, which `name` qualified with the mod's name names. While the flight display shows, `frame` draws it with this package's functions each frame, until it's turned off with `set_display_enabled`. A failed `frame` turns off that display only. `replaces` lists the game's instruments it stands in for, which aren't drawn while it's on, and `layout` moves and scales the instruments it names; they keep working, and go back as they were as soon as it's turned off, fails or its mod stops. Returns the qualified name.
    register_display: (name: string, definition: {frame: (seconds: number) -> (), replaces: { HudInstrument }?, layout: { [HudInstrument]: HudLayout }?}) -> string,
    -- Turns the display `name` on or off: the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one. Returns whether it's registered.
    set_display_enabled: (name: string, enabled: boolean) -> boolean,
    -- Where the game's instrument `instrument` last drew, in the window's pixels, as the mods' displays place it, and even while one stands in for it; nil before it first draws, or outside a mission.
    bounds: (instrument: HudInstrument) -> HudBounds?,
    -- How far the game's window that holds `instrument` has opened, and whether it's opening or closing, as the display last moved it on, which it does in every view; for the comms, the further open of its two windows. Nil for an instrument outside the windows, and outside a mission.
    window_state: (instrument: HudInstrument) -> HudWindowState?,
    -- Draws a PNG from the calling mod at `at`, with `size` in window pixels (nil uses its native size), tinted by `style`. Files are cached for the script context; one that hasn't been drawn for 2 frames makes room for others when the cache is full.
    picture: (at: vector, file: string, size: vector?, style: FillStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws shape `index` of the game's sprite set for this layer (the flight display's, or the front end screen's), with its anchor at `at`, in window pixels. The style's `scale` multiplies its size in the game's pixels.
    shape: (at: vector, index: number, style: ShapeStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws `text` at `at`, in pixels from the window's top left corner, in the game's font, as `style` says.
    text: (at: vector, text: string, style: TextStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws a line from `from` to `to`, in pixels, as `style` says.
    line: (from: vector, to: vector, style: LineStyle?) -> (),
    -- Fills the rectangle between the corners `from` and `to`, in pixels, as `style` says.
    rectangle: (from: vector, to: vector, style: FillStyle?) -> (),
    -- The size of `text` in window pixels, as `text` draws it: `style` is a text style, or just a number for its scale.
    measure: (text: string, style: (number | TextStyle)?) -> Size,
}

-- Drawing over the menus, the front end's screens and the pause menu, while they're shown: text, lines and rectangles, in the window's pixels.
type Ui = {
    -- Where the pointer is, in pixels from the window's top left corner, and whether its left button is down; nil before it has been over the window.
    pointer: Pointer?,
    -- Whether it's shown this frame, which is when what's drawn on it shows, and its other fields can be read.
    shown: boolean,
    -- The window's width, in pixels.
    width: number,
    -- The window's height, in pixels.
    height: number,
    -- Registers a screen, which `name` qualified with the mod's name names. While it's shown (`show_screen`), `frame` draws it with this package's functions each frame, and `key` gets each key as it goes down and up. Returns the qualified name.
    register_screen: (name: string, definition: {frame: (seconds: number) -> (), key: ((key: Key, down: boolean) -> ())?}) -> string,
    -- Makes the calling mod's registered screen `name` stand in for the front end's own `screen`, such as `"main_menu"`: while the front end shows `screen`, it runs and draws the mod's screen in its place, over its background. nil gives `screen` back to the front end. Only menu scripts can use it. Returns whether the screen is registered.
    replace_screen: (screen: FrontEndScreen, name: string?) -> boolean,
    -- Asks the front end to go to its screen `screen`, or to the mod's screen that stands in for it. Only menu scripts can use it.
    go_to: (screen: FrontEndScreen) -> (),
    -- Asks the front end to start the game mode `name`: the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one. Only menu scripts can use it. Returns whether the mode is registered.
    start_game_mode: (name: string) -> boolean,
    -- From a game mode's briefing screen, asks the front end to fly the mode's next mission (`core.game_mode_mission`). Only menu scripts can use it.
    launch_mission: () -> (),
    -- Plays the movie `name`, a Bink file of the game folder's or a mod's such as `"intro.bik"`, on a cleared screen, as the front end shows its next frame. Escape or the pointer's right button ends it. Only menu scripts can use it.
    play_movie: (name: string) -> (),
    -- Asks the front end to quit the game. Only menu scripts can use it.
    quit: () -> (),
    -- Shows the screen `name`: the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one. Nil closes the screen shown. Returns whether it's registered.
    show_screen: (name: string?) -> boolean,
    -- Draws a PNG from the calling mod at `at`, with `size` in window pixels (nil uses its native size), tinted by `style`. Files are cached for the script context; one that hasn't been drawn for 2 frames makes room for others when the cache is full.
    picture: (at: vector, file: string, size: vector?, style: FillStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws shape `index` of the game's sprite set for this layer (the flight display's, or the front end screen's), with its anchor at `at`, in window pixels. The style's `scale` multiplies its size in the game's pixels.
    shape: (at: vector, index: number, style: ShapeStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws `text` at `at`, in pixels from the window's top left corner, in the game's font, as `style` says.
    text: (at: vector, text: string, style: TextStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws a line from `from` to `to`, in pixels, as `style` says.
    line: (from: vector, to: vector, style: LineStyle?) -> (),
    -- Fills the rectangle between the corners `from` and `to`, in pixels, as `style` says.
    rectangle: (from: vector, to: vector, style: FillStyle?) -> (),
    -- The size of `text` in window pixels, as `text` draws it: `style` is a text style, or just a number for its scale.
    measure: (text: string, style: (number | TextStyle)?) -> Size,
}

-- Whether keys are held, and the controls bound to actions.
type Input = {
    -- Registers an action, which `name` qualified with the mod's name names, and which the controls screen lists by its `label` for the player to bind. A default key or button that's already taken stays unbound. Returns the qualified name, which `action_down` and `on_action` use. Only menu scripts can use it.
    register_action: (name: string, definition: ActionDefinition) -> string,
    -- Whether `key` is held down.
    key_down: (key: Key) -> boolean,
    -- Whether the controls bound to `action` are held: its key, or its joystick button. The action is one of the game's (`Action`), the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one.
    action_down: (action: string | number) -> boolean,
}

-- The camera's view: which it is, switching it, and registering views of the mod's own.
type Camera = {
    -- The view the camera shows: one of the game's (`View`), or a mod's by its qualified name; nil while no mission is shown.
    view: (string | number)?,
    -- Registers a camera view, which `name` qualified with the mod's name names. `frame` gives the camera's position and orientation each frame; its axes must be unit length, at right angles and right-handed. A failed `frame` goes back to the cockpit view. Returns the qualified name.
    register_view: (name: string, definition: {frame: (object: Object, seconds: number) -> {position: vector, orientation: Orientation}, letterbox: boolean?}) -> string,
    -- Switches to `view`, looking at `object`, or at the player's ship where it's nil. The view is one of the game's (`View`), the calling mod's by its own name, or any mod's by the qualified one. Returns whether it switched: a mission that holds the camera, or shows a cutaway, keeps it.
    set_view: (view: string | number, object: Object?) -> boolean,
}

-- Interface sounds, music and Betty's lines.
type Audio = {
    -- Plays sound `index` of the game's standard sounds, the menus' and the display's, at `volume` from 0 to 1, or at its loudest where it's nil. Returns whether it played.
    play_sound: (index: number, volume: number?) -> boolean,
    -- Plays the piece `name` from the game's music folder for ever, in place of the music playing.
    play_music: (name: string) -> (),
    -- Betty says `line`. Returns whether she does.
    say: (line: BettyLine) -> boolean,
}

-- Post effects: GLSL fragment shaders from the mod, drawn over the whole frame, before the flight display or after it.
type Postprocessing = {
    -- Registers a post effect: a GLSL fragment shader from the calling mod's file `shader`, drawn over the whole frame. `stage` is `"before_hud"` (the default) or `"after_hud"`, `order` sorts the effects of a stage, lower first, and `parameters` holds up to four numbers its shader reads. Returns the effect's name, qualified with the mod's. A shader that doesn't compile is an error, with its file and line. See the scripting guide for what the shader reads.
    register: (definition: Effect) -> string,
    -- Turns the calling mod's effect `name` on or off, by its own name or the qualified one. Returns whether the mod has it.
    set_enabled: (name: string, enabled: boolean) -> boolean,
    -- Sets the numbers the calling mod's effect `name` reads, up to four; those left out are 0. Returns whether the mod has it.
    set_parameters: (name: string, parameters: { number }) -> boolean,
}

-- Surface and lighting functions: GLSL functions from the mod that change how surfaces are lit.
type Shaders = {
    -- Registers a surface function: a GLSL function `surface` from the calling mod's file `shader`, which changes a pixel before it is lit. It draws on the `textures` it names, on every lit surface without a function of its own where `everywhere` is true, and on the objects given it with `object:set_surface`. `parameters` holds up to four numbers it reads. Returns the function's name, qualified with the mod's. A shader that doesn't compile is an error, with its file and line. See the scripting guide for what the function reads and sets.
    register_surface: (definition: SurfaceFunction) -> string,
    -- Registers a lighting function: a GLSL function `lighting` from the calling mod's file `shader`, which changes how much of each light reaches a pixel. One draws at a time: the enabled one registered last. `parameters` holds up to four numbers it reads. Returns the function's name, qualified with the mod's. A shader that doesn't compile is an error, with its file and line.
    register_lighting: (definition: LightingFunction) -> string,
    -- Turns the calling mod's function `name` on or off, by its own name or the qualified one. Returns whether the mod has it.
    set_enabled: (name: string, enabled: boolean) -> boolean,
    -- Sets the numbers the calling mod's function `name` reads on its textures and everywhere it draws, up to four; those left out are 0. Returns whether the mod has it.
    set_parameters: (name: string, parameters: { number }) -> boolean,
}

-- Sections of plain data for each mod: kept with the saved game, or in the game folder across every game.
type Storage = {
    -- The section `name` of the calling mod's storage that's kept with the saved game, and starts empty with each new game. Global and object scripts change it; other scripts read it.
    game_section: (name: string) -> Section,
    -- The section `name` of the calling mod's storage that's kept in the game folder, across every game. Any script changes it.
    global_section: (name: string) -> Section,
}

-- Timers, kept with the saved game: game time for global and object scripts, real time for player and menu scripts.
type Async = {
    -- Registers `handler` under `name`, for the timers of the mod's scripts of the same kind (and on the same object) to run. Register it as the script runs, so that a timer kept with a saved game finds it again after the game is loaded.
    register_timer: (name: string, handler: (data: any) -> ()) -> (),
    -- Runs the function registered under `name` once `seconds` have passed, with `data`, which must be plain data: seconds of game time for global and object scripts, and of real time for player and menu scripts.
    after: (seconds: number, name: string, data: any?) -> (),
}

-- Orientations, turning points between the world and an object's own frame, and angles. Luau's vector library has the rest of the vector maths.
type Util = {
    -- The point of the world that `point` is in the frame of something at `position` turned as `orientation`: `point`'s x to its right, y down and z forward of it.
    to_world: (position: vector, orientation: Orientation, point: vector) -> vector,
    -- Where the point of the world `point` is in the frame of something at `position` turned as `orientation`: x to its right, y down and z forward of it.
    to_local: (position: vector, orientation: Orientation, point: vector) -> vector,
    -- The angle in radians between the forward axis of something at `position` turned as `orientation` and the direction to `point`: 0 dead ahead, pi straight behind.
    angle_off: (position: vector, orientation: Orientation, point: vector) -> number,
    -- The orientation whose forward axis points along `direction`, turned about its Y axis, then its X axis, with no roll, as the game turns a ship to look at something.
    look_at: (direction: vector) -> Orientation,
    -- `orientation` turned by `angle` radians about its own `axis`: right-handed, so about its Y axis, which points down, a positive angle turns its nose to the right.
    turn: (orientation: Orientation, axis: Axis, angle: number) -> Orientation,
    -- The angles in radians that `orientation` is turned by from looking along the world's Z axis, as the game reads them: x the pitch, y the yaw and z the roll.
    angles: (orientation: Orientation) -> vector,
    -- The orientation turned by `angles` in radians from looking along the world's Z axis, as `angles` gives them: about X, then Y, then Z.
    from_angles: (angles: vector) -> Orientation,
    -- `angle` in radians brought within half a turn either way, from -pi to pi.
    normalize_angle: (angle: number) -> number,
}

-- Reading the game's and the mods' files.
type Vfs = {
    -- Reads the file `name` as a string of its bytes, the way the game does: a mod's copy (the latest mod's first), else the game folder's own file of that name, such as `missions\mission1.dte`, else the member of the game's `resource.hog`. Nil if there is none.
    read: (name: string) -> string?,
    -- Reads the calling mod's own file `name` as a string of its bytes. Nil if the mod doesn't have it.
    read_mod: (name: string) -> string?,
    -- Whether `vfs.read` would find the file `name`.
    exists: (name: string) -> boolean,
}

-- The options a mod offers the player on the mods screen: declaring the page, and reading the values.
type Options = {
    -- Declares the page of options the mod offers on the mods screen: a title and up to 64 options. Each option has a `key` that scripts read it by, a `label`, a `kind` and a `default`. A `"toggle"` has a boolean default. A `"choice"` has `choices`, each a `value` and a `label`, and a default among their values. A `"number"` has `min`, `max` and `step`, and a default in the range, and arrows step it. A `"slider"` is a number with a knob to drag, for a wide range. A `"text"` is a line the player types, of up to 24 characters, with a string default. A `"heading"` has only a `label`, and splits a long page. An option may have a `description`, which the screen writes under the list while the pointer is on it. Only load and menu scripts can use it, as OpenReliant starts, and a mod has one page.
    register_page: (page: Page) -> (),
    -- The value of the option `key` of the calling mod's page: what the player set, or the default. A toggle is a boolean, a number is a number, and a choice is the value of the choice set.
    get: (key: string) -> boolean | number | string,
    -- Sets the option `key` of the calling mod's page to `value`, as the player does on the mods screen: a toggle to a boolean, a number to a number, which is held to its range, a choice to one of its values, and a text to a string of up to 24 characters. The value is kept, and menu scripts hear of the change (`on_option_changed`).
    set: (key: string, value: boolean | number | string) -> (),
}

-- Lines and text placed in the world, drawn over the flight display where the camera sees them, for debugging.
type Debug = {
    -- Draws a line between the points `from` and `to` of the world, as `style` says, where the camera sees them.
    line: (from: vector, to: vector, style: LineStyle?) -> (),
    -- Draws `text` at the point `at` of the world, as `style` says, where the camera sees it.
    text: (at: vector, text: string, style: TextStyle?) -> (),
}

-- The interfaces other scripts offer, as `I.<name>`: those of the global scripts to global scripts, those of an object's scripts to the object's other scripts, and those of player and menu scripts to each other. Nil for one nobody offers.
type Interfaces = {
    Flight: {
        to_world: (position: vector, orientation: Orientation, point: vector) -> vector,
        to_local: (position: vector, orientation: Orientation, point: vector) -> vector,
